          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em lifting property
            is a property of a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphisms
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            within
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            to define properties of morphisms starting from an explicitly given class of morphisms. It appears in a prominent way in the theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            introduced by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Quillen"} Daniel Quillen
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+system"} factorization system
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
          %p#AsQualitativeNegation
            Often it is useful to think of lifting properties as a expressing a kind of qualitative negation (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+negation"}> Quillen negation
            ”): The morphisms with the left/right lifting property against those in a class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            tend to be characterized by properties opposite of those in
            = succeed "." do
