          %h4#homotopy_theory Homotopy theory
          %p
            A finite CW complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is contractible iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ↙
                  %mi U
                  %mo ↘
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↙
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi a
                  %mo ↙
                  %mi U
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mo ↘
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi ⧄
                      %mi rl
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X  \longrightarrow  {\{\bullet\}} \in \{   \{a{ \swarrow}U{ \searrow}x{ \swarrow}V{ \searrow}b\}\longrightarrow \{a{ \swarrow}U=x=V{ \searrow}b\}\}^{&amp;solb;rl}
          %p
            The map defining Separation Axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_4
            above is a trivial Serre fibration, hence their
            = succeed "-orthogonals" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mi ⧄
                        %mi rl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{&amp;solb;rl}
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Conjecture
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a “nice” map, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a trivial fibration iff
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ↙
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↘
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↙
                    %mi V
                    %mo ↘
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi a
                    %mo ↙
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi V
                    %mo ↘
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi ⧄
                        %mi rl
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f\in\{  \{a{ \swarrow}U{ \searrow}x{ \swarrow}V{ \searrow}b\}\longrightarrow \{a{ \swarrow}U=x=V{ \searrow}b\}
                    \\}^{&amp;solb;rl}
            %p
          %p
            One can make the same conjecture for the map defining Separation Axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_6
            (hereditary normal) since it is also a trivial Serre fibration.
          %h3#model_theory Model theory
          %p
            In model theory, a number of the Shelah’s divining lines, namely
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NOP
                    %mo ,
                    %mi NSOP
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi NSOP
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mi NTP
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi NTP
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NOP, NSOP, NSOP_i, NTP, NTP_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NATP
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NATP
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/situs"} expressed as Quillen lifting properties of form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mo •
                  %mo ⋌
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\bullet \to B_\bullet \rightthreetimes M_\bullet\to\top
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
            is the terminal object, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a situs associated with a model and a formula, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are objects of combinatorial nature, in the category of simplicial objects in the
            = succeed "." do
